The United Nations has declared 2018 as World Day Against Human Trafficking (WHDAT) in order to raise awareness on this issue. The day was set up by UN Secretary General Ban Ki-moon with support from UNICEF and other international organizations such as Amnesty International and Doctors Without Borders. This year marks its tenth anniversary since WHDAT began in 2007. In addition to raising public awareness, there are many ways you can help end trafficking in women and children worldwide. Here are some tips to get started:

* Donate blood if possible;
* Volunteer at your local hospital or clinic;
* Visit shelters where people may need them most;
* Share information online through social media platforms like Facebook, Twitter, Instagram etc.;
* Contact your government officials so they know what action needs to take against traffickers;
* Educate yourself on how to report suspected cases of child prostitution/trafficking;
* Get involved in advocacy efforts to protect vulnerable populations including girls under age 15 years old, pregnant women, victims of domestic violence, survivors of sexual abuse, refugees fleeing war zones, unaccompanied minors, sex workers, drug users, street vendors, beggars, homeless persons, orphans, widows, single mothers, abandoned babies, abused animals, pets, sick patients, elderly people, disabled individuals, prisoners, trafficked young adults, teenage boys, teenagers, prostitutes, pedophiles, pimps, criminals, terrorists, murderers, rapists, abusers, serial killers, kidnappers, armed robbers, bandits, thieves, smugglers, fugitives, illegal immigrants, undocumented migrants, asylum seekers, forced laborers, migrant farmworkers, foreign nationals working illegally, and others.
